\n**CASE:** TX-26-00875 | **DEFENDANT:** BENNIE CHARLES PATTERSON | **COURT:** 134th District Court, Judge Ginsberg\n\n---\n\n## PROPERTY & OWNER SITUATION\n\nDCAD account data unavailable in case file, but the $18,954.20 total debt amount indicates a modest-value property—likely improved residential in the $120,000-$180,000 range based on typical Dallas County tax rates running 2.5-3.0% of assessed value. Multiple defendants signal inheritance complications: BENNIE CHARLES PATTERSON (likely original owner/deceased), BENNY CHARLES PATTERSON II (son), PENNY PATTERSON (spouse/sibling), LANI PAIGE PATTERSON and PAUL WAYNE PATTERSON (additional heirs), plus TODD HUGHES (unrelated—possibly quitclaim recipient or tenant). The fact all parties except Paul Wayne were served by posting (courthouse bulletin board) on 06/23/2026 indicates abandonment or displacement. No actual physical addresses listed for any defendant screams probate-stalled inheritance with no party taking responsibility for the asset. Payment history shows complete non-engagement—nobody made even partial payments to prevent posting service. This is a classic "heir property" deadlock where multiple beneficiaries with fractional interests can''t coordinate action. Without DCAD lot/improvement split, assume structure play if in urban Dallas County core, land play if southern Dallas County fringe.\n\n## FINANCIAL PICTURE\n\nFiled debt shows $18,954.20 total, broken into Dallas County ($1,721.06), Parkland Hospital District ($1,814.78), Dallas College ($882.89), and Dallas County School Equalization Fund (amount truncated but approximately $14,535.47 by subtraction). The School Equalization portion being 77% of total debt is highly unusual—suggests either data error or special assessment accumulation over many years. Penalty/interest comprises roughly 42% of the entity amounts shown ($1,840.01 penalties on $2,577.72 in base taxes for the three complete line items), indicating 4-6 years delinquency minimum. Current live balance today (January 2025) is approximately $22,800-$24,100 after 7-8 additional months of 12% annual interest accrual plus potential 2025 tax year adding another $3,000-$4,500. True payoff cost runs $26,000-$28,500 once attorney fees (typically 20% = $3,790) are added. If property actually worth $150,000 market, debt-to-value sits at 18-19','\n',char(10)),NULL,'pre_judgment',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-07T22:45:58.932743','2026-07-07T22:45:58.932743','2026-07-07T22:45:58.932743',1,'individual','high','','',NULL,NULL);
