 years of non-payment given the multi-generational defendant list - estates that end up with 9 named parties don''t suddenly stop paying taxes last year; this is chronic neglect spanning likely 5-10+ years of delinquency.\n\n## 2. FINANCIAL PICTURE\n\n**Critical gap:** No tax balance, property ID, or appraised value provided in case data. However, the filing date of **06/02/2026** on a case that''s already moved to citation-by-posting (requested 06/05/2026, just 3 days post-filing) tells me the taxing authorities front-loaded their service efforts pre-filing, meaning delinquency is substantial enough to justify aggressive legal action. Typical Texas tax foreclosure cases hitting litigation involve **minimum $15K-$25K** in back taxes for residential properties, potentially $40K-$80K+ if this is commercial or multi-family. With 9 defendants and the estate complexity, I''d estimate we''re looking at **7-12 years of unpaid taxes** - Texas taxing entities usually don''t pursue fragmented estates unless the juice is worth the squeeze. Assuming a modest SF','\n',char(10)),NULL,'pre_judgment',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-07T22:56:36.908840','2026-07-07T23:24:03.377093','2026-07-07T23:24:03.377093',1,'individual','high','','',NULL,NULL);
